# How the Three UI Flows Work

---

## Flow 1 — Repository Files → File Cards

### Step 1: Analysis report arrives
`App` calls `analyzeRepository` (or `analyzeGitHubRepository`) via the API, which returns an `AnalysisReport` containing flat arrays of `GraphNode[]` and `GraphEdge[]`. Every node carries a `span.path` field that records which source file it belongs to.

### Step 2: Grouping nodes by file path
When the user switches to **File cards** view (`graphPresentation === "files"`), `GraphCanvas` calls `fileGroups()`. This function iterates every node, reads `nodePath(node)` (which returns `node.span?.path`), and buckets all nodes that share the same path into a `FileGroup`:

```ts
// GraphView.tsx:142–213
for (const node of nodes) {
  const path = nodePath(node);
  if (!path || node.kind === "repository") continue;
  const current = grouped.get(path) ?? [];
  current.push(node);
  grouped.set(path, current);
}
```

Each `FileGroup` has a `primary` node (the `module`-kind node for that file), a `members` list (symbols visible under the current node-kind filter), and a `uses` list (symbols from *other* files that this file references via non-`contains` edges).

### Step 3: External-package synthetic node
Before grouping, `externalPackageGraph()` scans `UnresolvedReference` entries whose `metadata.external === true` and synthesises a single virtual `GraphNode` (`id: "__waypoint_external_packages__"`) plus matching `imports` edges, so third-party dependencies appear as one extra card.

### Step 4: Dagre layout → React Flow nodes
`layoutFileGraph()` feeds each `FileGroup` path as a dagre node (sized by the chosen `FileCardSize`), runs `dagre.layout()` with `rankdir: "LR"`, then maps each group to a React Flow `Node` whose `data.label` is a JSX `<div className="file-graph-card">`. The card renders:
- A **heading** strip: detected language badge, filename, full path.
- A **members** section: one `<button>` per visible symbol (class, function, method). Clicking any button calls `onSelect({ type: "node", value: member })`.
- A **"Uses across files"** section listing symbols imported from other files.
- A **footer** counter: `N visible symbols · M external uses`.

```ts
// GraphView.tsx:280–340
<div className="file-graph-card">
  <div className={`file-card-heading …`}>…</div>
  <div className="file-card-members">
    {group.members.map((member) => (
      <button onClick={() => onSelect({ type: "node", value: member })}>…</button>
    ))}
    {group.uses.map((member) => (
      <button onClick={() => onSelect({ type: "node", value: member })}>…</button>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div className="file-card-footer">…</div>
</div>
```

The result is capped at 250 cards before layout runs.

---

## Flow 2 — Visible Graph Edges

Edge visibility is a two-stage filter applied entirely in the frontend; no extra API call is needed.

### Stage A: Kind filter (toolbar toggles)
`App` holds `visibleEdgeKinds: Set<EdgeKind>`, initialised to all four kinds (`contains`, `imports`, `may_call`, `instantiates`). Each toolbar button calls `toggleEdgeKind(kind)`, which adds or removes the kind from the set:

```ts
// App.tsx:358–365
const toggleEdgeKind = (kind: EdgeKind) => {
  setVisibleEdgeKinds((current) => {
    const next = new Set(current);
    if (next.has(kind)) next.delete(kind);
    else next.add(kind);
    return next;
  });
};
```

### Stage B: Node-membership filter (symbols mode)
In `GraphCanvas`, after the node list is trimmed to at most 400 by degree-centrality, edges are filtered to only those whose *both* endpoints are in the rendered set **and** whose kind is in `visibleEdgeKinds`:

```ts
// GraphView.tsx:534–539
const visibleEdges = props.edges.filter(
  (edge) =>
    props.visibleEdgeKinds.has(edge.kind) &&
    includedIds.has(edge.source) &&
    includedIds.has(edge.target),
);
```

### Stage C: Cross-file filter (files mode)
In `layoutFileGraph`, edges are additionally required to connect *different* file paths, both of which must be in the rendered group set:

```ts
// GraphView.tsx:232–244
for (const edge of graphEdges) {
  if (!visibleEdgeKinds.has(edge.kind)) continue;
  if (sourcePath === targetPath) continue;          // same-file edges dropped
  if (!includedPaths.has(sourcePath) || …) continue;
  const key = `${sourcePath}\0${targetPath}\0${edge.kind}`;
  groupedEdges.set(key, [...(groupedEdges.get(key) ?? []), edge]);
}
```

Multiple edges of the same kind between the same two files are collapsed into one React Flow edge; the count is shown as a label and the stroke width grows slightly with count (`1.5 + items.length * 0.12`). Visual encoding: `imports` edges are animated; `may_call` and `instantiates` use a dashed stroke; `contains` is thin and solid.

---

## Flow 3 — Selected Symbol → Inspector + Source Viewer

### Step 1: Selection is set
Clicking a symbol button inside a file card (or a node in symbols mode) calls `onSelect({ type: "node", value: member })`. In `App`, this handler sets `setSelection(nextSelection)` and clears `selectedFilePath`.

### Step 2: `activeSpan` is derived
`App` computes `activeSpan` from the current selection:

```ts
// App.tsx:151–162
const activeSpan = selectedSpan(selection) ?? (
  selectedFilePath ? { path: selectedFilePath, start_line: 1, … } : null
);
```

`selectedSpan` extracts `node.span` (for a node selection) or `edge.evidence.span` (for an edge selection).

### Step 3: Source file is fetched
A `useEffect` watches `activeSpan?.path`. When it changes, `App` calls `fetchSource(report.analysis_id, activeSpan.path)` → `GET /api/v1/analyses/{id}/source?path=…`. The response (`SourceDocument` with `content`, `language`, `path`, `line_count`) is stored in `source` state.

```ts
// App.tsx:274–301
useEffect(() => {
  if (!report?.analysis_id || !activeSpan) { setSource(null); return; }
  fetchSource(report.analysis_id, activeSpan.path)
    .then((document) => setSource(document))
    …
}, [report?.analysis_id, activeSpan?.path]);
```

### Step 4: `SourcePanel` renders Monaco and scrolls to the span
`SourcePanel` receives `source`, `span` (the `activeSpan`), and `theme`. It mounts a read-only Monaco editor. On mount and whenever `span` or `source` changes, `focusSpan()` runs:

1. Clamps `span.start_line` / `span.end_line` to the model's actual line count.
2. Calls `editor.setSelection(…)` to highlight the range.
3. Calls `editor.revealLineInCenter(startLine)` to scroll it into view.
4. Creates a decoration collection with CSS class `focused-source-line` (whole-line highlight) and `focused-source-gutter` (gutter marker).

```ts
// SourcePanel.tsx:25–57
const focusSpan = useCallback(() => {
  activeEditor.setSelection({ startLineNumber: startLine, … });
  activeEditor.revealLineInCenter(startLine, 0);
  decorationsRef.current = activeEditor.createDecorationsCollection([…]);
}, [span]);
```

A `requestAnimationFrame` + 80 ms `setTimeout` retry ensures the editor has rendered before the scroll fires.

### Step 5: `Inspector` shows evidence details
`Inspector` receives the same `selection`. When `selection.type === "node"`:
- **Details tab** (default): shows `node.signature`, `node.span.path`, line range, and stable node ID.
- **Usage tab**: lazily calls `fetchSymbolUsage(analysisId, node.id)` → `GET /api/v1/analyses/{id}/nodes/{nodeId}/usage`. The response lists incoming and outgoing `SymbolUsageRelationship` items (edge kind, direction, evidence span, resolution). Each is a clickable button that calls `onOpenNode(item.symbol.node_id)`.
- **Ask AI tab**: calls `askRepository(analysisId, question, [], conversationId, "inspector", node.id)` with the node ID as context, then renders the grounded answer with citation buttons that also call `onOpenNode`.

When `selection.type === "edge"`, the inspector shows the edge kind, source/target names, evidence status, confidence percentage, resolution text, observed syntax, and the exact file/line of the evidence span — all read directly from `edge.evidence`.

### Step 6: `onOpenNode` closes the loop
`openNodeFromWorkspace` in `App` looks up the node by ID in `report.nodes`, sets it as the new selection, and switches `workspaceMode` to `"explore"` — so clicking a citation or a usage relationship in the inspector navigates the whole workspace to that symbol.

```ts
// App.tsx:420–426
const openNodeFromWorkspace = (nodeId: string) => {
  const node = report?.nodes.find((item) => item.id === nodeId);
  if (node) {
    setSelection({ type: "node", value: node });
    setWorkspaceMode("explore");
  }
};
```

